\f1\fs22 The treatments Pin Cushion endured could manage symptoms and preserve function where possible, but none altered the direction of her disease\'97a version of muscular dystrophy called limb-girdle, because the weakness affected the muscles around the pelvis and shoulders. All the things a girl needs for getting up, climbing stairs, running, lifting her arms, and riding a bicycle that went nowhere. Pin Cushion\'92s was one of the nastier varieties; weakness could eventually involve the muscles that kept her breathing and her heart beating. Severe childhood-onset cases could lose the ability to walk before adulthood. Unlike Duchenne, the disease was not linked to X chromosomes, so it showed no particular preference for boys.\
\'93Sarco\'85 glycan\'85 opathy.\'94 Pin Cushion could name her illness since she was barely six years old. The part where she made it sound like three words, and the way she stressed 
\f3\i op
\f1\i0  with a little rise in pitch, had everyone she met wanting to hug her to health.\
Janine was working on TPlus, which gave her\'85 choices. As a biotech researcher, she ate from the fruit of the tree of knowledge every day, and she carried no illusions about the ethics grenades she routinely juggled.\
Pin Cushion never cried about any treatment. But she\'92d hug her mother every time after. Then they\'92d share a sweet little laugh, Janine would wipe away her tears, and carry on. Pin Cushion was twelve and deteriorating season-to-season. And now there was TPlus, still in clinical trials\'97a low-hanging fruit on the tree, right there in the lab.\
Whatever Janine decided, she\'92d be a monster, including the option where she dealt with it like everything else with Pin Cushion: she\'92d give her the data and wouldn\'92t sugar-coat it.\
Janine found Pin Cushion sitting on her bed, playing with her tablet. Her bedroom door was open; Janine wondered how much longer that would continue.\
Janine sat at the end of the bed, not too close, but near enough to reach out. She explained the drug she was developing at work, that it would probably, once approved, halt and even reverse Pin Cushion\'92s decline.\
\'93I\'92d be cured?\'94 Pin Cushion asked. \
\'93When can I start taking it?\'94\
\'93I can get you on a trial immediately.\'94 The words were heavy on Janine\'92s lips. Her daughter hugged her; Janine felt like she was watching it from the far corner of the room\'97from the ceiling.\
Pin Cushion sat back. \'93I suppose it\'92s an injection\'85\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93What if I stop taking it?\'94\
Janine relaxed. She could answer those kinds of questions while she assembled herself. \'93Most people will not want to stop, because of the way it rejuvenates and slows down ageing.  But if, say, a biologically rejuvenated sixty-year-old stops taking it, they don\'92t revert like a magic potion has worn off. Once TPlus is out of the system, ordinary ageing, genetics, diet, and exposure to infection can begin accumulating new disease.\'94\
\'93Keep taking the drug: got it.\'94\
Janine let that slide for now. \'93Cancer is\'85 never mind. Similar idea.\'94 Theoretically if TPlus destroys or suppresses every malignant clone, that cancer may be gone, but a person's ordinary lifetime cancer risk returns. Chronic diseases probably recur if TPlus is not present. Autoimmune disease, inherited metabolic defects, perhaps chronic inflammatory disorders: once TPlus protection falls away, the untreated disease mechanism would still be there. \'93In the case of muscular dystrophies, when TPlus repairs damaged tissue, that tissue doesn\'92t suddenly become damaged again.\'94\
Pin Cushion looked down at her legs. \'93Okay. So the sarcoglycanopathy is still there.\'94 This time, Pin Cushion didn\'92t kiddy the pronunciation down.\
\'93Yes.\'94\
Pin Cushion pulled herself off her bed and stretched. \'93So even if TPlus fixes the damage, the disease can start damaging my muscles again. How fast?\'94\
Janine wasn\'92t sure. \'93The same rate as before.\'94\
\'93Keep taking the drug: still got it.\'94\
Janine couldn\'92t let it pass through a second time. \'93For most patients, yes.\'94\
Pin Cushion dropped back onto the bed.\
Janine watched her settle. The simple act had required more care than it should have. Hands first, weight positioned, legs arranged afterwards.\
\'93Your disease presents an opportunity.\'94\
Pin Cushion looked suspicious.\
\'93Your muscles are continually being damaged and repaired. Damage, repair, damage, repair. TPlus is particularly effective in damaged tissue.\'94\
\'93So?\'94\
\'93So the normal treatment spreads a therapeutic dose throughout the body. In the case of muscular dystrophies, a much higher concentration can be administered directly into the affected muscles.\'94\
\'93Ouchies?\'94\
\'93Ouchies.\'94\
Pin Cushion narrowed her eyes.\
Janine went on. \'93I can\'92t give your whole body that much TPlus. Your kidneys, liver and heart would all get the same exposure. But I can put it straight into muscles that are already breaking and repairing, so the highest concentration is where it\'92s useful.\'94\
\'93So my rubbish muscles use it up?\'94\
\'93Not exactly. They give it somewhere useful to go.\'94\
\'93My rubbish muscles are useful.\'94\
\'93In this one very narrow and medically perverse sense.\'94\
Pin Cushion smiled. \'93\'91Perverse\'92 is a funny word.\'94\
\'93I guess it is.\'94\
Pin Cushion tried it on for size. \'93I have a perverse purse.\'94\
\'93I curse your perverse purse,\'94 Janine prompted.\
\'93Your curse is terse and worse than my perverse purse in a hearse.\'94\
\'93Disburse your perverse purse.\'94\
\'93Reverse your hearse.\'94\
\'93And call the nurse!\'94\
\'93Oh, tax! No more needles!\'94 Pin Cushion was permitted to swear about medical procedures.\
Mother and daughter fell about and laughed.\
Janine composed herself. \'93TPlus normally remains separate from your chromosomes. It enters the cells, does its work, but it doesn\'92t become part of you. Eventually it disappears.\'94\
\'93So\'85 injections forever.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93But?\'94\
Janine took a breath. \'93Cells have machinery that repairs damaged DNA. In ordinary tissue, the kind of damage and repair we need isn\'92t happening often enough. And at a safe whole-body dose, there isn\'92t enough TPlus around when it does.\'94\
Pin Cushion watched her.\
\'93Your muscles are different. The repair machinery is already busy. Constantly. And I can put the TPlus right beside it.\'94\
\'93It might use the TPlus in the repair.\'94\
Janine looked at her. \'93Yes.\'94\
\'93So it becomes part of me.\'94\
\'93That\'92s the possibility.\'94\
Pin Cushion sat forward.\
\'93Then I don\'92t have to keep taking it.\'94\
\'93That\'92s right, girl. If enough functional TPlus material became permanently incorporated, it could become self-sustaining.\'94\
\'93And keep fixing my muscles.\'94\
\'93That\'92s my reading of the evidence,\'94 Janine said.\
\'93So the thing breaking my muscles is also what lets you put the TPlus in permanently.\'94\
Janine looked at her.\
\'93Yes.\'94\
Pin Cushion considered this. \'93This isn\'92t some kind of zombie apocalypse scenario, is it? I mean, I don\'92t want to be dragging my mouldy arse around in three hundred years begging, 
\f3\i Brains! Brains!
\f1\i0 \'94\
\'93No, Pin Cushion, you won\'92t live 
\f3\i that 
\f1\i0 long.\'94\
\'93Aw. Shaved.\'94\
Janine looked at her daughter with a diagnostic eye.\
\'93It means 
\f3\i unfair
\f1\i0 , Mum.\'94
